---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro'
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro'
import Card from '../components/Card.astro'
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro'
import { yearsInBusiness } from '../lib/site'
import { breadcrumbs, collectionPage } from '../lib/schema'

/**
 * The migrations hub. The URL is deliberately `/website-migrations` rather than
 * `/migrations` — it carried real search impressions on the Webflow site and
 * the individual pages live under `/migrate/[slug]`, which also predate this
 * rebuild. See docs/seo-migration-map.md before renaming either.
 */
const migrations = (await getCollection('migrations')).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
)

const steps = [
  {
    title: 'We look at what you have',
    note: 'Every page, every URL, and what the site actually does. This is also where we tell you if moving is not worth it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Content and design come across',
    note: 'Rebuilt properly rather than converted, so you are not carrying over the compromises the old platform forced on you.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Every URL is mapped before launch',
    note: 'Old address to new address, one to one, live at the moment of the switch. This is the step that protects your rankings.',
  },
  {
    title: 'We watch it for a month',
    note: 'Search Console, redirects, and forms, checked after launch, not handed over and forgotten.',
  },
]

const schema = [
  collectionPage({
    name: 'Website migrations',
    description: 'Moving a website from one platform to another, with search rankings intact.',
    path: '/website-migrations',
    items: migrations.map((m) => ({ name: m.data.title, href: `/migrate/${m.id}` })),
  }),
  breadcrumbs([{ name: 'Website migrations' }]),
]
---

<Base
  title="Website Migration Services | Move Platforms Without Losing Rankings"
  description="We move websites between platforms including WordPress, Wix, Weebly, Shopify, Square, and Webflow, with every URL redirected and search rankings intact. Albuquerque, New Mexico."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="border-b border-line">
    <PageHero
      eyebrow="Website migrations"
      title="Moving platforms, without losing what you have earned"
      lede={`We have moved a lot of websites between platforms over ${yearsInBusiness} years. The part that matters is not the rebuild. It is the hundreds of small decisions that keep your search rankings, your content, and your customers' bookmarks working the day after the switch.`}
      ctas={[{ label: 'Book a free consultation', href: '/free-consultation' }]}
    />
  </div>

  <Section>
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="Common moves"
      title="Where most people are coming from"
      lede="Each of these covers what actually migrates, what you give up, how long it takes, and the cases where our honest answer is that you should stay where you are."
      class="mb-10"
    />

    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        migrations.map((m) => (
          <Card
            variant="service"
            tag={`${m.data.from} → ${m.data.to}`}
            title={m.data.title}
            blurb={m.data.summary}
            href={`/migrate/${m.id}`}
            cta="How it works"
          />
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 max-w-(--container-prose) text-ink-soft">
      Moving from something not listed, such as Squarespace, GoDaddy, Duda, or a site nobody has
      touched in a decade? We have almost certainly done it. <a
        href="/contact"
        class="font-bold text-primary no-underline hover:underline">Tell us what you are on</a
      > and we will tell you what it would take.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section variant="tint" width="full">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-(--container-content) px-6 py-14 md:px-10 lg:py-20">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="How it works"
        title="The same four steps every time"
        lede="A migration is mostly discipline. These are the steps that separate a move nobody notices from one that costs you six months of search traffic."
        class="mb-10"
      />
      <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-(--container-prose) flex-col">
        {
          steps.map((s, i) => (
            <div
              class="flex items-start gap-4 py-5 not-first:border-t not-first:border-line-soft"
              data-reveal
            >
              <span class="grid h-[38px] w-[38px] shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-paper-panel font-display text-base font-bold text-primary">
                {i + 1}
              </span>
              <div>
                <b class="mb-1 block text-[16.5px] font-extrabold text-ink">{s.title}</b>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">{s.note}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="The part that matters"
      title="Why rankings survive a proper migration"
      class="mb-6"
    />
    <div class="max-w-(--container-prose) text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <p class="mb-5">
        When a page moves and nothing tells search engines where it went, the value that
        page had built up is simply lost. Multiply that by a few hundred URLs and you have
        the migration horror stories people warn each other about.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-5">
        The fix is not clever. We export every URL from the existing site, map each one to
        its new home, and put those redirects live at the moment of the switch rather than
        the week after. Titles, descriptions, and structured data travel with the content.
        The old site stays reachable until DNS has fully propagated.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we watch Search Console for a month and fix what surfaces. The usual pattern
        is a small dip for a fortnight and then recovery, often to better than before,
        because the new site is faster. We did exactly this to <a
          href="/blog/rebuilding-our-own-website-2026"
          class="font-bold text-primary no-underline hover:underline">our own website</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section divider={false}>
    <CtaBand
      title="Stuck on a platform you have outgrown?"
      note="Tell us what you are on and what is frustrating you. We will tell you honestly whether moving is worth it."
    />
  </Section>
</Base>
